  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA sharp mid level trough will cross Lake Erie and northern Ohio \ntonight, with a cold pool aloft in the base of the trough dropping \n850MB temps down to -7C to -9C by late tonight. The cold air aloft \nwill aid in yielding moderate lake induced instability, with lake \ninduced equilibrium levels peaking over 10K feet. Despite the \ninstability, shallow synoptic scale moisture and short northwest \nfetch will limit the lake response to transient bands of light \nrain/snow showers tonight. The best coverage will be this evening \nwhen large scale ascent from DPVA/height falls ahead of the trough \ncross the eastern Great Lakes, with lake effect showers diminishing \novernight and then ending altogether Tuesday morning.\n\nPrecip type will change to mainly snow overnight with the exception \nof the immediate Lake Erie shore. The most organized area of lake \neffect showers will be tied to an upstream connection to Lake Huron. \nThis band will initially focus across northwest PA this evening, \nthen gradually move southwestward with time into northeast Ohio and \nthe eastern suburbs of Cleveland overnight through Tuesday morning. \nPrecipitation type will change to mainly snow across the somewhat \nhigher elevations inland from the lake overnight through Tuesday \nmorning, with some rain still mixing in along the immediate \nshoreline due to the warming influence of Lake Erie.\n\nExpect a spotty coating to an inch of accumulation overnight across \ninland Erie County PA and Crawford County PA, as well as inland \nAshtabula County OH. What remains of the band of lake effect will be \nnear the Cleveland Metro and points south Tuesday morning. A light \ncoating on the grass is possible south of Cleveland early before the \nlake effect ends by late morning. \n\nStrong high pressure will then build east across the eastern Great \nLakes Tuesday night through Wednesday night, with a period of dry \nand chilly weather for the busy pre-Thanksgiving travel period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nStrong northern and southern stream shortwaves will phase over the \nmidsection of the nation, with a deep closed low developing over the \nTennessee Valley by Thanksgiving Day. This deep closed low will then \nmove northeast, reaching New England by Saturday. A surface low will \ntrack northeast across the Ohio Valley to the eastern Great Lakes \nThursday through Friday, with a secondary low also moving NNE along \nthe eastern seaboard. A plume of deep Gulf and Atlantic moisture \nwill be captured by this system and advected northward into our \nregion, enhanced by a strong southeasterly low level jet. \n\nA solid area of rain will overspread the entire region from south to \nnorth Thanksgiving afternoon, with widespread rain lasting through \nthe first half of Thursday night. Friday through Saturday, \noccasional showers will continue as the deep mid level closed low \ncrosses the region. The airmass will grow cold enough for some lake \nenhancement as well by Friday and Saturday, with early indications \npointing towards westerly flow during this time frame.\n\nThe airmass will be warm enough for almost all rain through Friday \nmorning. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection will \ndrop 850MB temps down to -2C to -3C. Forecast soundings still \nsuggest rain much of the time, although some wet snow may mix in at \ntimes across the somewhat higher terrain of central Ohio into \nnorthwest PA. Surface and boundary layer temperatures are likely too \nwarm to allow for any accumulation during this period.\n\nA trough will persist over the Great Lakes Sunday through Monday, \nwith a few more rain and wet snow showers possible at times.",
